# Architecture Rules

Define the required backend architecture and layer boundaries.

## Runtime
- Node.js and CommonJS only; Express.js owns HTTP transport.
- Long-running work must run in worker or async job paths, not request threads.

## Layers
Order: `route -> controller -> service -> repository -> prisma`.
- Routes map URLs and middleware only.
- Controllers translate HTTP to service calls and response helpers only.
- Services own business logic, authorization, workflow orchestration, audit, and cross-module coordination.
- Repositories own all Prisma access; elsewhere it is forbidden except `prisma.$transaction(...)`.
- Emitted DTOs must expose `human_friendly_id` only; internal IDs never cross the HTTP or websocket boundary.
- Subscription, role, module, and ABAC checks run in middleware and re-validate in services for sensitive workflows.

## Modules
- One domain capability per module, with standalone workflows and explicit integration points.
- Services may depend on other services or shared libs, and must not on another module's controller or repository.

## Dependencies
- Dependency direction must move downward through the layer stack.
- Shared helpers live in `src/lib/*` only when stateless and reused across modules.
- Circular imports, reverse imports, and hidden side effects are defects.

## Lifecycle
- `src/server.js` owns startup and graceful shutdown, closing HTTP, Prisma, storage, and websocket resources.
- Startup diagnostics may fail fast when mandatory dependencies are unavailable.
